---
<DocsLayout title="Architecture" description="System architecture, request pipeline and the pure/adapter split" section="Architecture" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <h2 id="overview">Architecture Overview</h2>
  <p>
    The honeypot system is built on a layered architecture following a pure/adapter split pattern. All core
    logic lives in <code>*_rules.lua</code> modules with zero <code>ngx.*</code> dependencies, making them
    unit-testable under a plain Lua interpreter. Adapters do the I/O — Redis, Nginx, the file system,
    AbuseIPDB — and delegate every decision back to a pure rule module.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system can run as two hosts (edge and SIEM, the production shape) or as one host for evaluation.
    Topology does not change the detection logic; it only changes where telemetry is stored.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layers">Layers and Responsibilities</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <DocCard
      title="Edge proxy"
      href="/docs/detection"
      icon="shield"
      tag="Request path"
      description="OpenResty with Lua scoring, routing, session identity and pool assignment. The only component that sees client requests in plaintext."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Storefront and pools"
      href="/docs/engine"
      icon="box"
      tag="Deception path"
      description="A production WordPress/WooCommerce shop plus one cloned shop and database per honeypot session."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="State"
      href="#state"
      icon="database"
      tag="Redis"
      description="Session blobs, risk scores, routing decisions and pool assignments, with worker-local caches for hot paths."
    />
    <DocCard
      title="Telemetry"
      href="/docs/observability"
      icon="activity"
      tag="SIEM path"
      description="Structured security events and Suricata alerts shipped by Vector into Elasticsearch and rendered in Kibana."
    />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="pipeline">Request Pipeline</h2>
  <pre><code>Client Request → Nginx access_by_lua_block → pattern_analyzer.analyze_request()
  → router.decide_route() (12 stages) → pool_router.get_or_assign_pool()
  → session_handler.update_session() → Redis session_store
  → Suricata eve.json → init_worker.lua → threat_intel shared dict
  → abuseipdb_client.report_ip_async() → AbuseIPDB</code></pre>
  <p>The pipeline is deliberately synchronous in the decision path. Configured work that must not block the
  response — AbuseIPDB reporting, pool provisioning, content sync — is queued or handled by a worker.</p>

  <h3>Routing stages</h3>
  <p>The twelve stages are ordered from cheapest and most certain to most heuristic. A static asset is
  released immediately; a session already bound to a pool never has to be evaluated again for stickiness.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Condition</th><th>Route</th><th>Reason</th></tr>
    <tr><td>1</td><td>Static asset</td><td>Production</td><td>No attack surface</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1a</td><td>Consent-gated <code>gdpr_signature</code> match (returning honeypot visitor)</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>Known-bad browser re-consented</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1b</td><td>install.php on uninstalled WordPress</td><td>Production</td><td>Legitimate setup wizard</td></tr>
    <tr><td>2</td><td>Session already honeypot-bound</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>Sticky binding</td></tr>
    <tr><td>3</td><td>Risk score ≥ threshold</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>High risk</td></tr>
    <tr><td>4</td><td>CVE pattern matched</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>Known exploit attempt</td></tr>
    <tr><td>5</td><td>Vulnerable plugin URI</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>Direct plugin endpoint access</td></tr>
    <tr><td>6</td><td>Bad IP reputation (&gt; 50)</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>Flagged source</td></tr>
    <tr><td>7</td><td>Multiple admin attempts (≥ 3)</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>WSTG-ATHN-04</td></tr>
    <tr><td>8</td><td>Accumulated suspicious activity (≥ 5)</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>Cumulative signals</td></tr>
    <tr><td>9</td><td>Rapid automation (score ≥ 40)</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>WSTG-INFO-02</td></tr>
    <tr><td>10</td><td>Suspicious file upload</td><td>Honeypot</td><td>WSTG-INPV-10</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11</td><td>Default</td><td>Production</td><td>No rule fired</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="modules">Lua Modules</h2>
  <p>The system uses 16 pure modules and their adapter counterparts. Pure modules contain no <code>ngx.*</code> dependency and are unit-testable under a plain <code>lua</code> interpreter.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Pure Module</th><th>Adapter</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
    <tr><td>pattern_rules.lua</td><td>pattern_analyzer.lua</td><td>URI/header/CVE pattern scoring</td></tr>
    <tr><td>vulnerability_rules.lua</td><td>vulnerability_handler.lua</td><td>CVE/exploit-kit pattern matching</td></tr>
    <tr><td>suricata_rules.lua</td><td>init_worker.lua</td><td>eve.json alert parsing/scoring</td></tr>
    <tr><td>abuseipdb_rules.lua</td><td>abuseipdb_client.lua</td><td>Report eligibility logic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>router_rules.lua</td><td>router.lua</td><td>Routing predicates</td></tr>
    <tr><td>upload_rules.lua</td><td>upload_handler.lua</td><td>File-upload pattern analysis</td></tr>
    <tr><td>admin_rules.lua</td><td>admin_handler.lua</td><td>Admin-access decision logic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>session_rules.lua</td><td>session_handler.lua</td><td>Session anomaly detection</td></tr>
    <tr><td>decay_policy.lua</td><td>router_rules/suricata_rules</td><td>Escalation-aware decay</td></tr>
    <tr><td>lua_pattern_utils.lua</td><td>—</td><td>Shared string helpers</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ip_rules.lua</td><td>init.lua (utils)</td><td>IPv4/IPv6 CIDR matching</td></tr>
    <tr><td>honeytoken_rules.lua</td><td>honeytoken_handler.lua</td><td>Token-in-corpus matching</td></tr>
    <tr><td>gdpr_rules.lua</td><td>gdpr_handler.lua</td><td>Consent-gated identity</td></tr>
    <tr><td>shared_ip_rules.lua</td><td>pattern_analyzer.lua</td><td>Shared-address policy</td></tr>
    <tr><td>pool_router_rules.lua</td><td>pool_router.lua</td><td>Pool routing logic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>session_identity_rules.lua</td><td>session_handler.lua</td><td>Session cookie identity</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2 id="pooling">Pooling System</h2>
  <p>Each user session is permanently assigned to a specific honeypot pool instance (honeypot_eshop_N + honeypot_database_N). Pools are cloned from a pre-built template. Assignment is atomic via Redis EVAL. Scale-up and scale-down are managed by pool_manager.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Exclusive first.</strong> A free ready pool is claimed atomically, so two sessions cannot share an exclusive assignment.</li>
    <li><strong>Spare provisioning.</strong> Every assignment queues a wake-up, and the manager builds the next pool pair in parallel.</li>
    <li><strong>Borrow, then move.</strong> A waiting session borrows a ready pool and is moved when its own pool finishes building.</li>
    <li><strong>Bounded reuse.</strong> At the resource ceiling, sessions share ready pools round-robin instead of failing.</li>
    <li><strong>Idle retirement.</strong> Assignments expire after 24 hours; pools above the spare count are removed after the idle timeout.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See <a href="/docs/engine">Honeypot Engine</a> for the pool lifecycle, configuration knobs and isolation model.</p>

  <h2 id="state">State and Failure Modes</h2>
  <p>Redis holds the state that makes sessions sticky: session blobs, accumulated risk, offense counters and
  pool assignments. Nginx also keeps 10 MB worker-local shared dictionaries for sessions and threat
  intelligence, so hot paths avoid a round-trip when possible.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Redis unavailable.</strong> Session stickiness degrades; the router must prefer a safe production fallback rather than blocking the shop.</li>
    <li><strong>Pool unhealthy.</strong> The router falls back to the next healthy pool without changing the stored assignment.</li>
    <li><strong>Suricata silent.</strong> Application-layer scoring continues; only the network signal is lost.</li>
    <li><strong>SIEM unreachable.</strong> The edge keeps serving. Vector buffers and retries; detection is never coupled to Kibana availability.</li>
    <li><strong>Signing key unset.</strong> A random key is generated at startup; cookies from the previous process no longer verify. Set <code>SESSION_SIGNING_KEY</code> explicitly in production.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="gdpr">GDPR Identity</h2>
  <p>A privacy-preserving second identifier runs alongside the signed SERVERID session cookie. The browser RSA-OAEP-encrypts a payload of characteristics + timestamp into a gdpr_token. The proxy decrypts in memory, checks freshness, and hashes into a one-way gdpr_signature. Only the signature is stored (only when bound to a honeypot), with TTL as the binding's expiry.</p>
  <p>Consent gates the whole path: the token is only created after the visitor agrees, and the proxy never
  stores the plaintext payload. The signature is useful for recovering a returning attacker session; it is
  not a tracking identifier for legitimate visitors.</p>
</DocsLayout>
